                                                              "summary":  "Brown argues that current Wall Street skepticism about tech capex overbuilding fundamentally misreads the demand curve ahead. Tech leaders are planning infrastructure not for today\u0027s chatbots but for a future populated by billions of AI agents, autonomous robots, and fully automated operations ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Â a compute demand that dwarfs anything currently deployed. The piece contends that investors focused on near-term ROI questions are underestimating how discontinuous the shift to agentic AI will be.",
                                                              "take":  "Brown is making a variant of the \u0027picks and shovels\u0027 argument, but one level deeper: the shovel-makers are building for a gold rush that has not started yet, and that is exactly why the capex looks excessive in today\u0027s models. The risk to this thesis is timing ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Â if the agentic transition takes 8-10 years rather than 3-4, the current infrastructure build is simply early and the capital cost is real. The bull case requires trusting that hyperscaler executives have better demand visibility than public market analysts. Given that AWS, Azure, and Google Cloud have historically undersupplied capacity at inflection points, Brown\u0027s skepticism of the skeptics has historical backing ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Â but investors need to be honest that this is a multi-year thesis, not a near-term earnings catalyst.",
