                                                                                "summary":  "Damodaran examines the 2026 rate environment, where the 10-year Treasury climbed from 4.18% to 4.75% through August, yet both the S\u0026P 500 and NASDAQ rose significantly over the same period. He argues that higher rates do not mechanically mean lower stock prices -- the net effect depends on whether earnings growth and margin expansion can offset the higher discount rate. Corporate earnings surged over 11% in 2026, which absorbed most of the valuation headwind from rising rates. His key insight is that the rate-equity relationship is company-specific and context-dependent, not a simple inverse.",
