                                                                                "url":  "https://aswathdamodaran.blogspot.com/2026/09/the-scaling-and-profitability-trade-off.html",
                                                                                "date":  "2026-09-02",
                                                                                "imageUrl":  "https://blogger.googleusercontent.com/img/b/R29vZ2xl/AVvXsEgh5fXUk3rQgi_I28N1acK-5yXvwUPErlVboSe6pyCHZL8x0-r5qMMHlHdVhSxhBxFeuLGARgSFuwvCTDO_AdIgPsSjnpLeGDHHWmsXuT9hy7GID8Rkfe5QL_54HQTfOG1JUheL-vNqI2rSUlHl79wOqBiuwCGhvwRaQkpP2pQrPQ_PantALROtViRqHw/s320/Khoslatweet.jpg",
                                                                                "summary":  "Damodaran argues that VC\u0027s incentive structure -- driven by power-law return expectations and the pressure to price rather than value companies -- systematically pushes founders toward growth-at-all-costs strategies over sustainable profitability. Increased capital availability from public-market crossover investors has amplified this distortion, pushing companies to go public at massive valuations despite burning cash. He contends that scaling is only justified when the unit economics and market structure actually support it, but the VC ecosystem rarely enforces that discipline. The result is a recurring cycle of inflated valuations, disappointing IPOs, and post-listing crashes.",
                                                                                "take":  "Damodaran is identifying something structural, not cyclical. The VC model is essentially a tournament where only the top few outcomes matter, which creates rational incentives for individual managers to swing for the fences even when it destroys value for most companies in the portfolio. The AI wave is turbocharging this dynamic right now -- billions are flowing into startups whose scaling narratives are compelling but whose path to profitable unit economics is murky at best. Investors rotating into late-stage private or newly-public AI names should stress-test the profitability assumption hard before trusting the valuation.",
