                                                                                "title":  "We ain\u0027t seen nothing yet.",
                                                                                "source":  "Downtown Josh Brown",
                                                                                "author":  "Josh Brown",
                                                                                "url":  "https://www.downtownjoshbrown.com/p/we-ain-t-seen-nothing-yet",
                                                                                "date":  "2026-08-02",
                                                                                "imageUrl":  "https://media.beehiiv.com/cdn-cgi/image/format=auto,fit=scale-down,onerror=redirect/uploads/asset/file/edc330cd-79d0-4826-a3a5-bf12f41ad6a3/alex_kantrowitz.png?t=1785677394",
                                                                                "summary":  "Brown argues that Wall Street\u0027s anxiety over hyperscaler capex -- the hundreds of billions being spent on AI infrastructure by Microsoft, Google, Amazon, and Meta -- fundamentally misreads the scale of what\u0027s coming. He contends that a world with a billion people using personal AI assistants, plus autonomous vehicles and robots operating at scale, will demand multiples more compute than currently exists. Rather than representing overbuilding, today\u0027s data center and chip investment is simply trying to keep pace with inevitable future demand. The piece frames current skeptics as people anchored to today\u0027s technology who cannot envision the transformative capabilities emerging in the next few years.",
                                                                                "take":  "Brown is making the classic long-duration tech bull case, and it may be right -- but it has a timing problem. \u0027We\u0027ll need all this eventually\u0027 is not the same as \u0027this investment is correctly priced today.\u0027 The bull case for AI infrastructure capex requires that hyperscaler revenue from AI products scales fast enough to justify the capital before the next cycle of investor impatience kicks in. If monetization lags buildout by more than 18-24 months, the capex anxiety Wall Street has right now will look prescient, not myopic. The debate is not whether we need more compute -- it\u0027s whether the current pace of spending is disciplined or speculative.",
